  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nRelatively deep mixing is resulting in breezy south winds with\ngusts near 30 MPH across much of the area. Spotty gusts as high\nas 40 MPH cannot be ruled out through late afternoon. \n\nConvection is expected to develop along a pre-frontal trough\nover western Nebraska this evening, potentially reaching\nFurnas/Gosper/Dawson counties by around 7pm. A few of these\nstorms could be marginally severe through the evening, but\nintensity is expected to wane by midnight. The primary threat\nwould be wind given the deep mixing and high cloud bases. \n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms persist through the \novernight and into Tuesday morning as a cold front pushes in \nfrom the northwest. South of the front should remain mostly \nclear, allowing for one more day of heat index values near/over \n105 degrees. A Heat Advisory was issued for areas that are most \nfavored to remain south of the front into the afternoon, \nalthough there is some potential for this to \"bust\" if the \nfrontal timing were to speed up. Fun fact: this is the latest \nHeat Advisory ever issued by NWS Hastings. Prior to this year, \nwe had never issued a heat advisory in September, but there have\nbeen 4 days with Heat Advisories this September! \n\nMost of the storms during the daytime should be non-severe,\nalthough CAMs hint at slightly more robust convection along the\nfront late Tuesday afternoon into the evening. This would favor\nsoutheastern portions of the forecast area that are warmer and\ntherefore more unstable. \n\nWednesday and Thursday trend drier with more seasonable\ntemperatures (highs in the low 80s and lows in the upper 50s to\nlow 60s). That said, some low PoPs (10-20%) persist over\nportions of Kansas. Above-normal temperatures are favored to\nreturn Friday, but another system will provide a cooldown and\nadditional rain/t-storm chances for Saturday through next\nMonday.",
